276
00:12:02,800 --> 00:12:03,800
Yeah.

277
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:04,800
Okay.

278
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:05,800
Brilliant.

279
00:12:05,800 --> 00:12:06,800
So, let's move on to our third word.

280
00:12:06,800 --> 00:12:07,800
Marco, you already said our third word.

281
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:08,800
Oh, yeah.

282
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:09,200
I actually did.

283
00:12:09,800 --> 00:12:10,800
Brilliant.

284
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:12,320
That's a great word right there.

285
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:17,680
A lot of people might say, that's a great idea or great.

286
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:21,560
But in the dialogue, we heard another way of saying the same idea.

287
00:12:23,300 --> 00:12:25,220
That's a brilliant idea.

288
00:12:27,520 --> 00:12:29,760
That's a brilliant idea.

289
00:12:30,560 --> 00:12:30,760
Okay.

290
00:12:30,960 --> 00:12:31,780
So, this is great.

291
00:12:31,980 --> 00:12:35,380
But I would have to say that it's a little bit more British English.

292
00:12:35,800 --> 00:12:35,980
Yeah.

293
00:12:36,000 --> 00:12:36,880
I'd agree with you there.

294
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:42,860
But I also think it sort of gives more power to this idea that it's a good idea, brilliant idea.

295
00:12:43,600 --> 00:12:44,600
And it's a really, really good idea.

296
00:12:45,040 --> 00:12:45,200
Yeah.

297
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:46,840
Yeah.

298
00:12:46,840 --> 00:12:47,840
I like that word.

299
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:48,840
Yeah.

300
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:49,840
Okay.

301
00:12:49,840 --> 00:12:53,360
So, we're ready to listen to our dialogue again a third time, but at its normal speed.

302
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:04,160
All right, people.

303
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:10,280
We're holding this meeting today because we've got to do something about our sales and we need to do it now.

304
00:13:10,860 --> 00:13:12,240
I want concrete solutions.

305
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:14,260
How do you plan to drive sales?

306
00:13:14,700 --> 00:13:14,820
Roger.

307
00:13:15,950 --> 00:13:20,050
Well, in fact, we're the most expensive in the market.

308
00:13:20,210 --> 00:13:22,990
So maybe we need to lower our prices to match the competitors.

309
00:13:23,730 --> 00:13:24,690
Lower our prices.

310
00:13:25,250 --> 00:13:26,190
Not very creative.

311
00:13:26,430 --> 00:13:27,330
It'll never fly with Swan.

312
00:13:27,910 --> 00:13:28,890
What kind of thinking is that?

313
00:13:29,010 --> 00:13:29,190
Geez.

314
00:13:29,830 --> 00:13:30,750
Anyone have a better plan?

315
00:13:31,310 --> 00:13:31,910
Natalie?

316
00:13:32,510 --> 00:13:34,230
Perhaps a promotion.

317
00:13:34,910 --> 00:13:37,590
Maybe a two-for-one offer or something like that.

318
00:13:37,970 --> 00:13:39,230
That's the same thing.

319
00:13:39,810 --> 00:13:40,330
Bad idea.

320
00:13:40,750 --> 00:13:41,650
Really bad idea.

321
00:13:42,210 --> 00:13:42,890
Damn it, people, come on.

322
00:13:43,350 --> 00:13:43,390
Think.

323
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:46,040
The CEO will be here any minute.

324
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:49,240
Hello, Mr. Swan.

325
00:13:49,960 --> 00:13:50,660
Oh, Mr. Swan.

326
00:13:50,900 --> 00:13:51,580
Hello, Mr. Swan.

327
00:13:51,640 --> 00:13:52,680
Do we have any ideas yet?

328
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:53,800
Yes, Mr. Swan.

329
00:13:53,940 --> 00:13:57,180
We were kind of considering a two-for-one offer to get more competitive.

330
00:13:57,940 --> 00:13:59,580
A two-for-one promotion?

331
00:14:00,060 --> 00:14:00,420
Hmm.

332
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:02,020
I kind of like the sound of that.

333
00:14:02,300 --> 00:14:03,820
It sounds like something we should consider.

334
00:14:03,980 --> 00:14:05,040
Yeah, exactly!

335
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:06,560
That's just what I was thinking.

336
00:14:06,880 --> 00:14:08,120
In fact, it's a brilliant idea.

337
00:14:08,260 --> 00:14:09,040
I'm glad we thought of that.

338
00:14:09,360 --> 00:14:09,920
Very creative.

339
00:14:15,690 --> 00:14:16,290
All right.

340
00:14:16,520 --> 00:14:19,370
So listening to this dialogue makes you think about this boss.

341
00:14:19,750 --> 00:14:24,950
I'm pretty sure everyone has had a manager or a supervisor that's similar to this guy.

342
00:14:25,230 --> 00:14:31,270
Yeah, this boss, Clark, I would have to say he sounds like a terrible person to work for.

343
00:14:31,990 --> 00:14:32,690
Yeah, that's true.

344
00:14:32,850 --> 00:14:35,210
If you're a manager, you want to motivate your employees.

345
00:14:35,650 --> 00:14:44,010
I think it's the most important thing a manager does is to encourage his or her employees and to help support them to grow and to be better people in the job.

346
00:14:44,290 --> 00:14:46,030
And this guy is totally not doing this.

347
00:14:46,150 --> 00:14:52,850
And you can tell because they are a little bit scared of talking and he cuts them off with saying, oh, it's a bad idea.

348
00:14:53,110 --> 00:14:54,950
Give me a better idea and stuff like that.

349
00:14:55,090 --> 00:14:57,150
Yeah, I could never work for a guy like this.

350
00:14:57,490 --> 00:15:08,450
You know, my first boss that I had when I first entered the workforce, I was in a very junior position and I was working for a large company and the general manager of the company, he was amazing.

351
00:15:08,870 --> 00:15:10,070
He was so encouraging.

352
00:15:10,650 --> 00:15:18,530
He knew every single employee's name, first name, and he would make an effort to stop and say hello and ask them how things were going.

353
00:15:19,680 --> 00:15:20,680
And you know what?

354
00:15:20,680 --> 00:15:27,110
This left me with such a good feeling about the company that I really wanted to work hard for this company and try my best.

355
00:15:27,400 --> 00:15:30,550
And I think that I've never met such a great boss as this guy.

356
00:15:30,830 --> 00:15:31,810
No, I think that's true.

357
00:15:32,360 --> 00:15:40,680
Especially if you have a boss that's encouraging and motivating like that, it makes you work harder and makes you feel part of a really special team.

358
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:42,320
And that's something we can all agree on.

359
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:44,780
And I'm sure all our managers out there do the same.

360
00:15:45,180 --> 00:15:45,660
Well, you know what?

361
00:15:45,780 --> 00:15:54,900
I hope some of our listeners log on to our website and maybe they can tell us some experiences with bad managers or good managers or if they are managers, how they handle things.

362
00:15:55,080 --> 00:15:55,240
Yeah.

363
00:15:55,280 --> 00:15:59,280
And hopefully they can use some of these great phrases that we talked about today.

364
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:00,040
Great.

365
00:16:00,760 --> 00:16:10,600
And be sure everyone to visit our website at EnglishPod.com and where you can find a lot of other information and really good resources to continue improving your English.

366
00:16:10,600 --> 00:16:10,720
That's right.

367
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:15,380
So thanks for listening today everyone and until next time, this is goodbye.

368
00:16:15,600 --> 00:16:24,999
Bye.
